<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Klement Gottwald</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1896">1896</a>–<a href="/years/1953">1953</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Klement Gottwald was leader of the Communist Party of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a> from 1929. He became prime minister after the party's success in the 1946 elections, led the communist seizure of power in February 1948 and succeeded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edvard-benes">Edvard Beneš</a> as president that June. His rule brought Stalinist purges and show trials, and he died in March 1953.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/klement-gottwald.jpg" alt="Klement Gottwald" width="320" height="442" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown · CC BY-SA 3.0 de · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_183-R90009,_Budapest,_II._Weltfestspiele,_Festumzug,_tschechische_Delegation_(cropped_KG).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Gottwald was born on 23 November 1896, either at Dědice, now part of Vyškov, or at Hoštice-Heroltice. His mother was an unmarried maidservant. He trained in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> as a carpenter. He served in the Austro-Hungarian Army from 1915 to 1918 and deserted in the summer of 1918. In the 1920s he edited communist magazines in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovakia">Slovakia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ostrava">Ostrava</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>From 1926 Gottwald was a functionary of the Communist Party of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a> (KSČ). He helped form a pro-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> opposition within the party and from 1928 was a member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communist-international">Comintern</a>. At the Fifth Congress in February 1929 he was elected general secretary. In 1938 he led opposition to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich-agreement">Munich Agreement</a>. After the party was banned he emigrated to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> in November 1938. In 1943 he agreed with the government-in-exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> and President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edvard-benes">Edvard Beneš</a> to unite resistance in a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-front-czechoslovakia">National Front</a>.</p><p>Gottwald returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a> on 10 May 1945 as deputy premier. In the same year he became party chairman. The KSČ won 38 percent in the free election of 1946, the best result of any Czechoslovak party to that point, and he became prime minister. By the summer of 1947 its popularity had fallen, particularly after the Soviets pressed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a> to refuse <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marshall-plan">Marshall Plan</a> aid, and Stalin ordered Gottwald to act against parliamentary opposition. In February 1948 twelve non-Communist ministers resigned over the Communist interior minister Václav Nosek's packing of the police. Gottwald refused to resign and threatened a general strike, and on 25 February Beneš accepted the resignations. Beneš resigned on 2 June, and Gottwald was elected president on 14 June.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After a meeting with Stalin, Gottwald imposed the Soviet model: industry was nationalised and the collectivisation of farms began. Resistance in the government was met with purges, and perceived opponents were jailed or sentenced to forced labour. Show trials included that of the non-Communist politician Milada Horáková, executed in June 1950, and of the Communist leaders <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rudolf-slansky">Rudolf Slánský</a> and Vlado Clementis, both executed in December 1952.</p><p>Gottwald died on 14 March 1953, five days after returning from Stalin's funeral, and was the first Czechoslovak president to die in office. Antonín Zápotocký succeeded him as president and Antonín Novotný as party leader. His embalmed body was displayed in a mausoleum in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a> until 1962, and his ashes were moved in 1990 to a common grave at Olšany Cemetery. The city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zlin">Zlín</a> was named Gottwaldov from 1949 to 1989.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Klement Gottwald” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Communist Party of <b>Klement Gottwald</b>, the largest party in the 1946 elections, took full power in February 1948.</blockquote><a href="/empire/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Dědice, in what is now <a href="/countries/czech-republic">the Czech Republic</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/czech-republic">the Czech Republic</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=49.29,16.98&amp;year=1933" aria-label="Dědice on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4941 445 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Klement Gottwald"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5391 715h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5391 715h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=49.29,16.98&amp;year=1933">What was here in 1933? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
